DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The SHA256_ functions calculate a 256-bit cryptographic checksum (digest)
     for any number of input bytes.  A cryptographic checksum is a one-way
     hash function; that is, it is computationally impractical to find the in‐
     put corresponding to a particular output.  This net result is a
     “fingerprint” of the input-data, which does not disclose the actual in‐
     put.

     The SSHHAA225566__IInniitt(), SSHHAA225566__UUppddaattee(), and SSHHAA225566__FFiinnaall() functions are the
     core functions.  Allocate an _S_H_A_2_5_6___C_T_X, initialize it with
     SSHHAA225566__IInniitt(), run over the data with SSHHAA225566__UUppddaattee(), and finally ex‐
     tract the result using SSHHAA225566__FFiinnaall(), which will also erase the
     _S_H_A_2_5_6___C_T_X.

     SSHHAA225566__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA225566__FFiinnaall() which converts the return
     value to a 65-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII string
     which represents the 256 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA225566__FFiillee() calculates the digest of a file, and uses SSHHAA225566__EEnndd() to
     return the result.  If the file cannot be opened, a null pointer is re‐
     turned.  SSHHAA225566__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() is similar to SSHHAA225566__FFiillee(), but it only cal‐
     culates the digest over a byte-range of the file specified, starting at
     _o_f_f_s_e_t and spanning _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes.  If the _l_e_n_g_t_h parameter is specified
     as 0, or more than the length of the remaining part of the file,
     SSHHAA225566__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() calculates the digest from _o_f_f_s_e_t to the end of file.
     SSHHAA225566__DDaattaa() calculates the digest of a chunk of data in memory, and
     uses SSHHAA225566__EEnndd() to return the result.

     When using SSHHAA225566__EEnndd(), SSHHAA225566__FFiillee(), or SSHHAA225566__DDaattaa(), the _b_u_f argu‐
     ment can be a null pointer, in which case the returned string is allo‐
     cated with malloc(3) and subsequently must be explicitly deallocated us‐
     ing free(3) after use.  If the _b_u_f argument is non-null it must point to
     at least 65 characters of buffer space.

     SHA224 is identical SHA256, except it has slightly different initializa‐
     tion vectors, and is truncated to a shorter digest.

EERRRROORRSS
     The SSHHAA225566__EEnndd() function called with a null buf argument may fail and
     return NULL if:

     [ENOMEM]           Insufficient storage space is available.

     The SSHHAA225566__FFiillee() and SSHHAA225566__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() may return NULL when underlying
     open(2), fstat(2), lseek(2), or SHA256_End(3) fail.

